messages:  ["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nA street comprehensive management committee has a total of 6 members: F, G, H, I, M and P. Each of these members shall serve as a member of at least one of the three sub-committees under the Comprehensive Management Committee. Each sub-committee consists of three different members. The known information is as follows: one of the six members serves as a member of three subcommittees. F is not a member of the same subcommittee as G. H is not a member of the same subcommittee as I.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following options must be true?\n# Options:\nA) One of f or g is a member of three sub-committees.\nB) One of h or i is a member of three sub-committees.\nC) P or m is a member of only one sub-committee.\nD) One member happens to be a member of the two sub-committees.\n<think>\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, let's break down the given information and analyze each option step by step.\n\n1. **Total Members and Sub-committees**: There are 6 members (F, G, H, I, M, P) and 3 sub-committees, each consisting of 3 members.\n2. **One Member in Three Sub-committees**: One of the six members serves as a member of three sub-committees.\n3. **Constraints on Memberships**:\n   - F is not a member of the same subcommittee as G.\n   - H is not a member of the same subcommittee as I.\n\nLet's analyze each option:\n\n**Option A: One of f or g is a member of three sub-committees.**\n- This option is not necessarily true. The problem states that one of the six members serves as a member of three sub-committees, but it does not specify whether this member is F or G. Therefore, we cannot conclude that one of F or G must be the member in three sub-committees.\n\n**Option B: One of h or i is a member of three sub-committees.**\n- Similar to Option A, this option is not necessarily true. The problem states that one of the six members serves as a member of three sub-committees, but it does not specify whether this member is H or I. Therefore, we cannot conclude that one of H or I must be the member in three sub-committees.\n\n**Option C: P or m is a member of only one sub-committee.**\n- This option is not necessarily true. The problem does not provide any specific information about the number of sub-committees P or M are members of. They could be members of one, two, or three sub-committees.\n\n**Option D: One member happens to be a member of the two sub-committees.**\n- This option is true. Given that there are 3 sub-committees and 6 members, and one member is a member of three sub-committees, it is inevitable that at least one member must be a member of two sub-committees. This is because if each of the three sub-committees had exactly one member, that would account for only 3 members, leaving 3 members unaccounted for. Since one member is already a member of three sub-committees, at least one of the remaining members must be a member of two sub-committees.\nThe correct option is:"]
