messages:  ["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nA certain meeting invited six people from a certain workplace: Zhang, Wang, Li, Zhao, Sun, and Zhou to attend. However, due to work arrangements, all six people cannot go to the meeting at the same time. Among them: Only one person between Zhang and Zhao can attend the meeting; Also Only one person between Li and Zhao can attend the meeting; At least one person between Zhang and Wang can attend the meeting; Two of the three among Zhang, Sun, and Zhou can attend the meeting; Wang and Li either both attend the meeting or neither of them attend the meeting; if Zhao will not attend the meeting, then Sun will not attend the meeting either.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, according to this arrangement, Which one probably will not attend the meeting in the end?\n# Options:\nA) Zhang and wang\nB) Wang and li\nC) Li and zhao\nD) Zhao and sun\n<think>\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, let's break down the given conditions and analyze them step by step:\n\n1. **Only one person between Zhang (Z) and Zhao (Zh) can attend the meeting.**\n   - This means either Z or Zh can attend, but not both.\n\n2. **Only one person between Li (L) and Zhao (Zh) can attend the meeting.**\n   - This means either L or Zh can attend, but not both.\n\n3. **At least one person between Zhang (Z) and Wang (W) can attend the meeting.**\n   - This means either Z or W can attend, but not both.\n\n4. **Two of the three among Zhang (Z), Sun (S), and Zhou (Zo) can attend the meeting.**\n   - This means exactly two out of Z, S, and Zo can attend.\n\n5. **Wang (W) and Li (L) either both attend the meeting or neither of them attend the meeting.**\n   - This means W and L must either both be included or both be excluded.\n\n6. **If Zhao (Zh) will not attend the meeting, then Sun (S) will not attend the meeting either.**\n   - This means if Zh is not attending, then S must also not be attending.\n\nNow, let's analyze the options given:\n\n**Option A: Zhang and Wang (Z, W)**\n- If Z and W both attend, then from condition 3, W and Z cannot both attend, which contradicts our assumption. Therefore, Z and W cannot both attend.\n\n**Option B: Wang and Li (W, L)**\n- From condition 5, W and L must either both attend or both not attend. If W and L both attend, then from condition 3, W and Z cannot both attend, which is possible. However, if W and L both attend, then Z and S must not attend (from condition 4), and if Zh does not attend (from condition 2), then S must not attend (from condition 6). This would mean Z, W, and L all attend, which contradicts condition 3. Therefore, W and L cannot both attend.\n\n**Option C: Li and Zhao (L, Zh)**\n- From condition 2, L and Zh cannot both attend. If L and Zh both attend, then from condition 1, Z and Zh cannot both attend, which is possible. However, if L and Zh both attend, then Z and S must not attend (from condition 4), and if Zh does not attend (from condition 2), then S must not attend (from condition 6). This would mean Z, L, and Zh all attend, which contradicts condition 3. Therefore, L and Zh cannot both attend.\n\n**Option D: Zhao and Sun (Zh, S)**\n- From condition 6, if Zh does not attend, then S must not attend. If Zh attends, then from condition 2, L and Zh cannot both attend, which means L must not attend. If L does not attend, then from condition 4, Z and S must attend. This means Z, S, and Zh all attend, which contradicts condition 3. Therefore, Zh and S cannot both attend.\nThe correct option is:"]
