messages:  ["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nThere are six switches S, T, W, X, Y, and Z in a circuit. The following conditions must be met to use these switches: (1) If W is turned on, then X should also be turned on; (2) Only when S is turned off , thenT can also be turned off; (3) T and X cannot be turned on at the same time, nor can they be turned off at the same time; (4) If Y and Z are turned on at the same time, W must also be turned on.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, if both S and Z are now turned on, which of the followings must be true?\n# Options:\nA) T is turned on and y is turned off.\nB) Both t and y are turned off.\nC) Both w and t are turned on.\nD) X is turned on and y is turned off.\n<think>\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, let's break down the given conditions and analyze the implications step by step:\n\n1. **Condition 1**: If W is turned on, then X should also be turned on.\n   - This means W and X must be either both on or both off.\n\n2. **Condition 2**: Only when S is turned off, then T can also be turned off.\n   - This implies that if S is on, T must also be on. Conversely, if T is on, S must be on.\n\n3. **Condition 3**: T and X cannot be turned on at the same time, nor can they be turned off at the same time.\n   - This means T and X must be in opposite states (one on and one off).\n\n4. **Condition 4**: If Y and Z are turned on at the same time, W must also be turned on.\n   - This means Y and Z being on together implies W must be on.\n\nNow, let's analyze the given scenario where both S and Z are turned on:\n\n- Since S is on, from Condition 2, T must also be on.\n- Since T is on, from Condition 3, X must be off.\n- Since X is off, from Condition 1, W must be off.\n- Since W is off, from Condition 4, Y cannot be on (because Y and Z being on together would force W to be on).\n\nNow, let's evaluate the options:\n\nA) **T is turned on and Y is turned off.**\n   - This is true based on our analysis (T is on and Y cannot be on).\n\nB) **Both T and Y are turned off.**\n   - This is false because T is on.\n\nC) **Both W and T are turned on.**\n   - This is false because W is off.\n\nD) **X is turned on and Y is turned off.**\n   - This is false because X is off.\nThe correct option is:"]
