messages:  ['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nThe principle of utmost good faith in insurance means that both parties to the insurance must be honest and trustworthy during the entire process of signing and performing the insurance contract, abide by the trust with the utmost sincerity, truthfully inform important information, do not deceive or conceal, and ensure the correct performance of their respective rights And obligations.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, in which of the following, the applicant has not fulfilled the principle of utmost good faith?\n# Options:\nA) Liu took a medical leave due to liver cancer. in july 2005, liu\'s decoration company insured group life insurance for all employees of the company. when the company submitted the list of insureds, it stated in the column of health status that liu took a leave of liver cancer. condition\nB) Mr. zhang, in good health, has purchased a simple life insurance under the collective organization of the unit, and fills in the word health in the column health of the insured on the insurance application form\nC) In the new semester, the school asked freshmen to pay an insurance premium of 25 yuan each, of which student safety insurance was 10 yuan, and additional disease insurance was 15 yuan. on september 8, a new student zhang had abdominal pain and was diagnosed with a congenital disease by the hospital. zhang was hospitalized, and zhang\'s father asked the insurance company to reimburse the medical expenses\nD) Li was hospitalized with gastric cancer, was discharged after the operation, and went to work normally. later, li was introduced by someone to insured for simple life insurance, and when filling out the insurance form, he did not declare the fact that he was suffering from cancer\n<think>\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, we need to carefully analyze each option and determine whether the applicant has fulfilled the principle of utmost good faith in the context of insurance. The principle of utmost good faith requires honesty and truthfulness in the application process and the disclosure of all relevant information. Let\'s examine each option step by step:\n\nA) Liu took a medical leave due to liver cancer. In July 2005, Liu\'s decoration company insured group life insurance for all employees of the company. When the company submitted the list of insureds, it stated in the column of health status that Liu took a leave of liver cancer. Condition:\n- Here, Liu\'s company provided a truthful and accurate health status (liver cancer leave) when submitting the list of insureds. This action aligns with the principle of utmost good faith.\n- Therefore, option A does not indicate a failure to fulfill the principle of utmost good faith.\n\nB) Mr. Zhang, in good health, has purchased a simple life insurance under the collective organization of the unit, and fills in the word "health" in the column health of the insured on the insurance application form.\n- Mr. Zhang, being in good health, truthfully indicated his health status as "health" on the application form.\n- This action also aligns with the principle of utmost good faith.\n- Therefore, option B does not indicate a failure to fulfill the principle of utmost good faith.\n\nC) In the new semester, the school asked freshmen to pay an insurance premium of 25 yuan each, of which student safety insurance was 10 yuan, and additional disease insurance was 15 yuan. On September 8, a new student Zhang had abdominal pain and was diagnosed with a congenital disease by the hospital. Zhang was hospitalized, and Zhang\'s father asked the insurance company to reimburse the medical expenses.\n- This scenario involves a situation where the student was hospitalized due to a congenital disease, which was not disclosed on the insurance application form.\n- The father of the student did not disclose this information to the insurance company, which is a breach of the principle of utmost good faith.\n- Therefore, option C indicates a failure to fulfill the principle of utmost good faith.\n\nD) Li was hospitalized with gastric cancer, was discharged after the operation, and went to work normally. Later, Li was introduced by someone to insured for simple life insurance, and when filling out the insurance form, he did not declare the fact that he was suffering from cancer.\n- Li did not disclose his cancer diagnosis when applying for the simple life insurance, which is a breach of the principle of utmost good faith.\n- Therefore, option D indicates a failure to fulfill the principle of utmost good faith.\n\nAfter analyzing all the options, we can conclude that options C and D both indicate a failure to fulfill the principle of utmost good faith. However, the question asks for the option where the applicant has not fulfilled the principle of utmost good faith, implying a single correct answer. Given the context and the options provided, the most straightforward and clear failure is in option C, as it involves a situation where the student\'s health status was not disclosed, leading to potential misrepresentation of the risk profile.\nThe correct option is:']
