      "text": " D) 75%<|User|>Got it. Let's move on to the next problem.\n# Context:\nA survey was conducted among 1000 people to determine their favorite ice cream flavor. The results showed that 40% preferred chocolate, 35% preferred vanilla, and 25% preferred strawberry.\n\n# Question:\nIf the survey was repeated with 2000 people, and the same preferences were observed, what would be the expected number of people who preferred chocolate?\n# Options:\nA) 400\nB) 500\nC) 600\nD) 800\n<|Assistant|> <think>\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, let's break down the information and calculate the expected number of people who preferred chocolate in the new survey.\n\n1. **Context Analysis:**\n   - In the original survey of 1000 people, 40% preferred chocolate.\n   - The same preferences (40% chocolate, 35% vanilla, 25% strawberry) are observed in the new survey with 2000 people.\n\n2. **Calculate the expected number of people who preferred chocolate in the new survey:**\n   - The percentage of people who preferred chocolate remains the same at 40%.\n   - For the new survey with 2000 people, the expected number of people who preferred chocolate is calculated as follows:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Expected number of people who preferred chocolate} = 40\\% \\times 2000\n     \\]\n   - Convert the percentage to a decimal for calculation:\n     \\[\n     40\\% = 0.40\n     \\]\n   - Perform the multiplication:\n     \\[\n     0.40 \\times 2000 = 800\n     \\]\n\n3. **Option Analysis:**\n   - A) 400\n   - B) 500\n   - C) 600\n   - D) 800\n\nBased on the calculations, the expected number of people who preferred chocolate in the new survey with 2000 people is 800.\n\nThe correct option is:\n<|Assistant|> D) 800<|User|>Alright, let's tackle the next problem.\n# Context:\nA survey was conducted among 1000 people to determine their favorite ice cream flavor. The results showed that 40% preferred chocolate, 35% preferred vanilla, and 25% preferred strawberry.\n\n# Question:\nIf th