messages:  ["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nSome studies claim that cancer cells are afraid of heat, and high body temperature can fight cancer. The most cancer-prone organs of the human body, including the lungs, stomach, large intestine, breast, etc., are all parts of the body with low body temperature, and high temperature organs such as the heart are not easy to get cancer. Therefore, you can  raise your body temperature to fight cancer by doing exercise, drinking hot water, bathing and other ways.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the followings, if true, can best refute the above assertion?\n# Options:\nA) Affected by breathing, diet, etc., the temperature of the human mouth is generally lower than that of the rectum, but the incidence of rectal cancer worldwide is higher than that of oral cancer.\nB) The human body temperature has a precise regulation mechanism, which is basically stable, and there is no obvious temperature difference between the various organs in the body.\nC) Hyperthermia may help radiotherapy or some chemotherapy to play a better role, but research data to prove its reliability is still insufficient.\nD) Malignant tumors rarely occur in the heart because the cardiomyocytes here no longer divide or proliferate, regardless of the temperature.\n<think>\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, we need to identify which option best refutes the assertion that raising body temperature can fight cancer. Let's analyze each option step by step:\n\nA) This option suggests that the temperature of the human mouth is generally lower than that of the rectum, but the incidence of rectal cancer is higher than oral cancer. This does not directly address the relationship between body temperature and cancer risk. It only provides a comparison between two different body parts and their cancer incidences.\n\nB) This option states that the human body temperature has a precise regulation mechanism and there is no obvious temperature difference between various organs. This directly challenges the assertion by suggesting that body temperature is not a significant factor in cancer risk. If body temperature is precisely regulated and does not vary significantly between organs, then it cannot be a major factor in cancer development.\n\nC) This option mentions that hyperthermia may help radiotherapy or chemotherapy, but the reliability of this method is still insufficient. While this provides some information about the potential benefits of hyperthermia, it does not directly refute the assertion that raising body temperature can fight cancer. It merely suggests that more research is needed.\n\nD) This option states that malignant tumors rarely occur in the heart because cardiomyocytes do not divide or proliferate, regardless of temperature. This provides a specific reason why the heart is less prone to cancer, but it does not directly address the assertion about raising body temperature. It only explains a particular case.\nThe correct option is:"]
