ove them out of the solar system.\"\n     - This option contradicts the first conclusion, which states that Jupiter's gravity either pushes them into smaller orbits or expels them.\n   - Option C: \"Jupiter's gravity pushed them into smaller orbits, but did not drive them out of the solar system.\"\n     - This option contradicts the second conclusion, which states that Jupiter's gravity either pushes them into smaller orbits or drives them out of the solar system.\n   - Option D: \"Jupiter's gravitational pull neither pushed them into smaller orbits nor drove them out of the solar system.\"\n     - This option contradicts both conclusions, as it implies that Jupiter's gravity does not affect the orbits or the solar system status of the objects.\n\nGiven the analysis, the correct option is:\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>B) Jupiter's gravity did not push them into smaller orbits, but drove them out of the solar system.<\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the detailed explanation. I understand the reasoning now. To confirm, the correct answer is B, right?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Yes, the correct answer is B. According to the context and the two researchers' conclusions, if only one of the assertions is true, then the statement \"Jupiter's gravity did not push them into smaller orbits, but drove them out of the solar system\" is the only one that can be inferred, as it does not contradict either conclusion while the other options do.<\uff5cUser\uff5c>Great, I understand now. Thank you for walking me through this logical reasoning problem step by step.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! If you have any more questions or need further explanations, feel free to ask. Understanding these logical reasoning problems is a valuable skill. Good luck with your studies!<",
