    {
      "index": 0,
      "text": "C) Nothing venture, nothing gain.\n\nLet's analyze each option:\n- **A) Respect the elderly and parents**: This is a learned behavior and societal expectation, not an innate instinct. It is acquired through socialization and cultural norms, fitting the definition of acquisition motivation.\n- **B) Beautiful and gentle girls are loved by boys**: This is a learned preference based on societal standards and cultural values. It is not an innate instinct but rather a learned behavior.\n- **C) Nothing venture, nothing gain**: This is an expression of caution and prudence, often attributed to the principle of risk aversion. It is a common piece of advice that people often follow, but it is not an innate instinct. It is more of a learned behavior derived from experience and societal norms.\n- **D) Respect teachers and education, respect literature and martial arts**: Similar to option A, this is a learned behavior and societal expectation. It is not an innate instinct but rather a learned behavior through education and cultural influence.\n\nGiven the definitions, the option that most likely belongs to the original motivation (primitive motive) is C) Nothing venture, nothing gain, as it represents a principle of caution and prudence that is often seen as a natural inclination rather than a learned behavior.\n</think>\n<answer>C</answer>",
