messages:  ['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nInvisible curriculum refers to the informal and unconscious learning experience that is not clearly specified in the school\'s planned curriculum.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, according to the above definition, which of the following is not an invisible curriculum?\n# Options:\nA) Colleges and universities organize students to participate in two-day military training to cultivate and exercise students\' willpower.\nB) When children learn teaching courses, they cultivate their interest in learning and teaching.\nC) A university set up a university culture festival to let students know the school motto and style of study.\nD) Teachers and students participate in extracurricular activities together to establish an equal and harmonious relationship between teachers and students.\n<think>\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, we need to understand the definition of an "invisible curriculum" and then evaluate each option to determine which one does not fit this definition. Let\'s break it down step by step:\n\n1. **Definition of Invisible Curriculum**: \n   - The invisible curriculum refers to the informal and unconscious learning experiences that are not explicitly part of the planned curriculum.\n   - It includes the values, norms, and social interactions that students pick up through their experiences within the school environment.\n\n2. **Analyzing Each Option**:\n   - **Option A**: "Colleges and universities organize students to participate in two-day military training to cultivate and exercise students\' willpower."\n     - This option describes a structured, planned activity (military training) that is part of the formal curriculum. Therefore, it is not an example of an invisible curriculum.\n   - **Option B**: "When children learn teaching courses, they cultivate their interest in learning and teaching."\n     - This option describes a planned course (teaching courses) where students learn about teaching and develop an interest. Again, this is part of the formal curriculum, not an invisible curriculum.\n   - **Option C**: "A university set up a university culture festival to let students know the school motto and style of study."\n     - This option describes a planned event (culture festival) aimed at teaching students about the school\'s values and style of study. It is part of the formal curriculum, not an invisible curriculum.\n   - **Option D**: "Teachers and students participate in extracurricular activities together to establish an equal and harmonious relationship between teachers and students."\n     - This option describes informal, unplanned interactions (extracurricular activities) that occur outside the formal curriculum. These interactions can shape students\' perceptions and relationships, fitting the definition of an invisible curriculum.\n\n3. **Conclusion**:\n   - Based on the analysis, options A, B, and C all describe planned, formal activities that are part of the curriculum. Only option D describes an informal, unplanned interaction that aligns with the definition of an invisible curriculum.\nThe correct option is:']
