ials are being targeted. It only mentions a general trend in corruption complaints, which does not weaken the argument about political targeting.\n\nOption B states that almost all of the officials who have served in city government for any length of time are appointees of Mayor Bixby. This option actually supports the argument by reinforcing the idea that most officials are appointed by Mayor Bixby, making it more likely that they would be targeted for political reasons.\n\nOption C states that Former Mayor Bixby did not publicly oppose Mayor Stephens' establishment of the anticorruption task force. This option does not provide any information about why the officials are being targeted. It only mentions that Mayor Bixby did not oppose the task force, which is not relevant to the argument about political targeting.\n\nOption D states that all of the members of the anticorruption task force had other jobs in city government before the task force was formed. This option provides an alternative explanation for why the officials are being targeted. If the task force members had other jobs, it suggests that their involvement in the task force is not necessarily due to their political affiliations with Mayor Bixby, but rather due to their other city government roles. This weakens the argument that the officials are being targeted because of their political affiliations.\nThe correct option is:"]
