messages:  ['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nLance: If experience teaches us nothing else, it teaches us that every general rule has at least one exception. Frank: What you conclude is itself a general rule. If we assume that it is true, then there is at least one general rule that has no exceptions. Ttherefore, you must withdraw your conclusion.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, frank\'s argument is an attempt to counter Lance\'s conclusion by\n# Options:\nA) Demonstrating that lance assumes the very thing he sets out to prove\nB) Establishing that experience teaches us the opposite of what lance concludes\nC) Showing that lance\'s conclusion involves him in a contradiction\nD) Showing that it has no implications for any real cases\n<think>\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, let\'s break down the context and the argument step by step.\n\n1. **Understanding Lance\'s Argument:**\n   - Lance states: "If experience teaches us nothing else, it teaches us that every general rule has at least one exception."\n   - This means that for any general rule, there is always an exception.\n\n2. **Understanding Frank\'s Argument:**\n   - Frank counters Lance by saying: "What you conclude is itself a general rule. If we assume that it is true, then there is at least one general rule that has no exceptions."\n   - Frank is essentially saying that if Lance\'s conclusion (that every general rule has at least one exception) is true, then there must be at least one general rule that has no exceptions.\n\n3. **Analyzing the Options:**\n   - **Option A: Demonstrating that Lance assumes the very thing he sets out to prove**\n     - Frank does not argue that Lance assumes the opposite of what he concludes. Instead, Frank is arguing that if Lance\'s conclusion is true, it leads to a contradiction.\n   - **Option B: Establishing that experience teaches us the opposite of what lance concludes**\n     - Frank does not argue that experience teaches the opposite of Lance\'s conclusion. Frank is not discussing the nature of experience but rather the logical implications of Lance\'s conclusion.\n   - **Option C: Showing that lance\'s conclusion involves him in a contradiction**\n     - Frank\'s argument does involve a contradiction. If Lance\'s conclusion is true, then there must be at least one general rule with no exceptions, which contradicts Lance\'s statement that every general rule has at least one exception.\n   - **Option D: Showing that it has no implications for any real cases**\n     - Frank\'s argument does have implications for real cases. Frank is arguing that if Lance\'s conclusion is true, it leads to a specific contradiction.\nThe correct option is:']
