ly mentions a change in traffic deaths among older people.\nB) For the population as a whole, annual alcohol consumption is no lower now than it was in 1990.\n- This option confirms that overall alcohol consumption has not decreased, which aligns with the survey results. However, it does not explain why traffic deaths have decreased.\nC) The proportion of people under 21 who own a car is higher now than it was in 1990.\n- This option suggests that more young people own cars, which could lead to more alcohol-related traffic deaths if they drive under the influence. However, it does not explain why the decrease in traffic deaths occurred.\nD) Alcohol consumption away from home, for example in bars and restaurants, is much lower among people under 21 than it was in 1990.\n- This option provides a crucial insight. If young people are consuming less alcohol away from home and more at home, it suggests that they are drinking less alcohol overall. This could explain why traffic deaths have decreased despite the same level of alcohol consumption. The decrease in traffic deaths might be due to a reduction in the number of young people driving under the influence, rather than an increase in overall alcohol consumption.\n</think>\n<answer>\nThe correct option is:"]
