messages:  ['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nFor example, X X Group held an anniversary celebration, your mobile phone number won a grand prize of 100000 yuan. this kind of unwise fraudulent text messages on mobile phones still appear again and again even after they have been exposed by the media. Professional swindlers prefer to use shoddy fraudulent text messages rather than design more deceptive and gullible text messages, which only shows that the swindlers are too stupid and unprofessional.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following options, if true, strongly refutes the above conclusion?\n# Options:\nA) A swindler must be smart, otherwise it is impossible to deceive others.\nB) When swindlers cheat, they will try their best not to attract the attention of the police.\nC) If a deception was useless, the swindler would have eliminated it long ago.\nD) Swindlers use such text messages to catch people who may be fooled, and they want to filter out smart people in the first place.\n<think>\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, let\'s break down the context and the conclusion, and then analyze each option to determine which one strongly refutes the given conclusion. \n\nThe context states that fraudulent text messages are still prevalent despite being exposed by the media, and professional swindlers prefer to use shoddy fraudulent text messages rather than more deceptive ones. This implies that swindlers are either too stupid or unprofessional to create more sophisticated scams.\n\nThe conclusion drawn from this context is that swindlers are too stupid and unprofessional.\n\nNow, let\'s analyze each option to see which one refutes this conclusion:\n\nA) "A swindler must be smart, otherwise it is impossible to deceive others."\n- This option directly contradicts the conclusion by stating that swindlers need to be smart to deceive others. If swindlers need to be smart, it implies that they are not necessarily stupid or unprofessional, which directly refutes the conclusion.\n\nB) "When swindlers cheat, they will try their best not to attract the attention of the police."\n- This option discusses the swindlers\' strategy to avoid detection but does not address their intelligence or professionalism. It does not directly refute the conclusion about their stupidity or unprofessionalism.\n\nC) "If a deception was useless, the swindler would have eliminated it long ago."\n- This option suggests that swindlers would eliminate ineffective methods. However, it does not address the intelligence or professionalism of the swindlers. It only implies that they are efficient in eliminating ineffective methods, which does not directly refute the conclusion.\n\nD) "Swindlers use such text messages to catch people who may be fooled, and they want to filter out smart people in the first place."\n- This option suggests that swindlers target gullible people but does not directly address their intelligence or professionalism. It implies that swindlers are aware of the target audience but does not refute the conclusion about their stupidity or unprofessionalism.\nThe correct option is:']
