that if both parents have type O blood, their children will have type O blood. The student\'s example contradicts this.\n   - **Option B:** "People with type O blood can\'t have children with type B blood."\n     - This option is not directly addressed by the professor\'s statement. The professor\'s statement does not specify what happens when one parent has type O and the other has type B.\n   - **Option C:** "People with type B blood will always have children with type O blood."\n     - This option is not supported by the professor\'s statement. The professor\'s statement does not claim that type B parents will always have type O children.\n   - **Option D:** "If both parents have type B blood, their children will also have type B blood."\n     - This option is not supported by the professor\'s statement. The professor\'s statement does not make any claims about type B parents having type B children.\n\n4. **Conclusion:**\n   - The student\'s statement directly contradicts the professor\'s statement by providing an example where both parents have different blood types (type B and type O) and the child has type O blood.\n   - The student\'s understanding aligns most closely with Option A, which states that only those with type O blood have children with type O blood.\nThe correct option is:']
