messages:  ["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nEditorial: Our society has a vested interest in maintaining a political system in which candidates are free to adhere to their principles. Yet campaigning for elected office is extremely costly, and because only the wealthiest individuals are able to finance their own political campaigns, most candidates must seek funding from private sources. In so doing, the candidates are almost invariably obliged to compromise their principles. Thus, government itself should assume the cost of candidates' campaigns.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which one of the following principles, if valid, most helps to justify the conclusion as it is drawn in the argument?\n# Options:\nA) Candidates should not run for elected office if doing so would compel the candidates to compromise their principles.\nB) Voters should not support a candidate if that candidate is known to have accepted funding from private sources.\nC) Candidates wealthy enough to finance their own political campaigns should not be permitted to raise additional funds from private sources.\nD) The government should finance a given activity if doing so will further a vested interest of society.\n<think>\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, let's break down the context and the question step by step. The context presents an argument that suggests the government should assume the cost of candidates' campaigns to prevent candidates from compromising their principles due to the high cost of campaigning. The question asks which principle, if valid, most helps to justify this conclusion.\n\nFirst, let's analyze the argument:\n1. The argument states that candidates must seek funding from private sources to campaign.\n2. Private funding often leads to candidates compromising their principles.\n3. Therefore, the government should assume the cost of candidates' campaigns to prevent this compromise.\n\nNow, let's examine each option:\n\nA) Candidates should not run for elected office if doing so would compel the candidates to compromise their principles.\n- This option is about candidates not running for office, which is not directly related to the argument about the government funding campaigns. It doesn't support the conclusion that the government should fund campaigns.\n\nB) Voters should not support a candidate if that candidate is known to have accepted funding from private sources.\n- This option is about voter behavior and does not address the justification for the government funding campaigns. It doesn't support the conclusion either.\n\nC) Candidates wealthy enough to finance their own political campaigns should not be permitted to raise additional funds from private sources.\n- This option is about limiting private funding for wealthy candidates, which is not directly related to the argument about the government funding campaigns. It doesn't support the conclusion.\n\nD) The government should finance a given activity if doing so will further a vested interest of society.\n- This option directly aligns with the argument. The argument suggests that the government should fund campaigns to further a vested interest of society (maintaining a political system where candidates adhere to their principles). This principle supports the conclusion that the government should fund campaigns.\nThe correct option is:"]
