ess of the method, it does not directly address the core mechanism of preventing typhoons from gathering energy and moving forward.\n   - **Option B**: "Artificial clouds will affect rainfall in adjacent areas"\n     - This option discusses the potential impact on rainfall in adjacent areas, which is not directly related to the prevention of typhoons gathering energy and moving forward.\n   - **Option C**: "When typhoon passes by, it is often accompanied by strong convective weather such as strong wind and rainstorm"\n     - This option describes a common occurrence during typhoons but does not provide a necessary condition for the proposed method to work.\n   - **Option D**: "Driving force of typhoon comes from the heat generated by sunlight on the surface of sea water"\n     - This option directly addresses the core mechanism of typhoons. If the driving force of typhoons is indeed the heat generated by sunlight on the surface of sea water, then preventing typhoons from gathering enough energy by reflecting sunlight would logically prevent them from forming or gathering strength.\n\n4. **Conclusion**:\n   - The prerequisite that must be true for the conclusion to hold is that the driving force of typhoons is indeed the heat generated by sunlight on the surface of sea water. This is because if this condition is met, then preventing typhoons from gathering enough energy by reflecting sunlight would logically prevent them from forming or gathering strength.\nThe correct option is:']
