messages:  ['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nRaymond Burr played the role of Perry Mason on television. Burr\' s death in 1993 prompted a prominent lawyer to say, Although not a lawyer, Mr. Burr strove for such authenticity that we feel as if we lost one of our own.  This comment from a prestigious attorney provides appalling evidence that, in the face of television, even some legal professionals are losing their ability to distinguish fiction from reality.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, the reasoning in the argument is flawed because the argument\n# Options:\nA) Presumes that the lawyer is qualified to evaluate the performance of an actor\nB) Takes the views of one lawyer to represent the views of all lawyers\nC) Criticizes the lawyer rather than the lawyer\'s statement\nD) Ignores the part of the lawyer\'s remark that indicates an awareness of the difference between reality and fiction\n<think>\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, let\'s break down the context and the question step by step.\n\n1. **Context Analysis**:\n   - Raymond Burr played the role of Perry Mason on television.\n   - Burr\'s death in 1993 prompted a prominent lawyer to comment.\n   - The lawyer\'s comment suggests that Burr\'s performance was so authentic that it made the audience feel as if they were losing one of their own.\n   - The lawyer then states that this loss of authenticity is a problem, implying that even some legal professionals are losing their ability to distinguish fiction from reality.\n\n2. **Question Analysis**:\n   - The question asks us to identify the flaw in the argument based on the given context.\n   - We need to evaluate each option to determine which one correctly identifies the flaw.\n\n3. **Option Analysis**:\n   - **Option A**: "Presumes that the lawyer is qualified to evaluate the performance of an actor"\n     - This option suggests that the lawyer\'s qualification to evaluate an actor\'s performance is questionable. However, the context does not provide any information about the lawyer\'s qualifications to evaluate acting performances.\n   - **Option B**: "Takes the views of one lawyer to represent the views of all lawyers"\n     - This option suggests that the argument generalizes the lawyer\'s opinion to all lawyers. The context does not provide any information about the lawyer\'s status or whether their opinion is representative of other lawyers.\n   - **Option C**: "Criticizes the lawyer rather than the lawyer\'s statement"\n     - This option suggests that the argument focuses on criticizing the lawyer rather than addressing the lawyer\'s statement. However, the context does not indicate that the argument is criticizing the lawyer.\n   - **Option D**: "Ignores the part of the lawyer\'s remark that indicates an awareness of the difference between reality and fiction"\n     - This option suggests that the argument overlooks the part of the lawyer\'s statement that acknowledges the difference between reality and fiction. The lawyer\'s comment acknowledges that Burr\'s performance was so authentic that it made the audience feel as if they were losing one of their own, implying that the performance was very realistic. However, the lawyer then criticizes this authenticity, suggesting that it is problematic.\nThe correct option is:']
