messages:  ['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nWittgenstein was a student of Moore, a famous philosopher at Cambridge University. One day, philosopher Russell asked Moore, who is your best student? Moore said without hesitation: Wittgenstein. why? because among all the students, only he always showed a blank look when listening to the class, and there were always endless questions. later, Wittgenstein is more famous than Russell. Someone asked, why did Russell fall behind? Wittgenstein said: he has no problem.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, this paragraph is intended to illustrate that:\n# Options:\nA) Doubting and questioning are always the secret of success\nB) Curiosity is the internal driving force of research\nC) Appreciation is an important factor in success\nD) Finding problems is the premise of continuous progress\n<think>\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, let\'s carefully analyze the context and the question provided. The context describes a conversation between Russell, Moore, and Wittgenstein, highlighting their views on Wittgenstein\'s qualities and success. The key points are:\n1. Wittgenstein was described by Moore as the best student because he always showed a blank look and had endless questions.\n2. Wittgenstein is more famous than Russell.\n3. Russell, when asked why Wittgenstein was his best student, said Wittgenstein had no problem.\nFrom these points, we need to determine which option best captures the main message of the paragraph.\n</think>\n<answer>\nLet\'s evaluate each option:\nA) Doubting and questioning are always the secret of success: This option aligns with the idea that Wittgenstein\'s endless questions and blank looks were seen as positive attributes. However, the paragraph does not explicitly state that these qualities are the "secret" of success.\nB) Curiosity is the internal driving force of research: While curiosity is mentioned (endless questions), the paragraph does not focus on research specifically. It emphasizes Wittgenstein\'s behavior in class rather than his research.\nC) Appreciation is an important factor in success: This option is not supported by the context. The paragraph does not mention anyone appreciating Wittgenstein or his work.\nD) Finding problems is the premise of continuous progress: This option aligns well with the context. Wittgenstein\'s blank looks and endless questions indicate that he was constantly finding problems and seeking solutions, which is a key aspect of continuous progress and improvement.\nGiven the analysis, the most appropriate option is D.\n</answer>\nThe correct option is:']
