eople quit smoking.\n\n3. **Option Analysis**:\n   - **A) Regular exercise prevents weight gain.**\n     - This option supports the argument. If exercise prevents weight gain, it could be a reason why participants in the second group (who also exercised) were more likely to quit smoking.\n   - **B) Each group in the study included four hundred randomly selected participants.**\n     - This option provides information about the sample size but does not directly support the argument about the effectiveness of exercise in quitting smoking.\n   - **C) Nonsmokers accustomed to regular exercise do not gain weight when they stop exercising.**\n     - This option supports the argument. If exercise prevents weight gain, it could explain why participants in the second group were less likely to gain weight and thus more likely to quit smoking.\n   - **D) Aerobic exercise can stimulate the brain's production of endorphins, which reduce tension.**\n     - This option supports the argument. If exercise reduces tension, it could explain why participants in the second group were more likely to quit smoking due to reduced stress.\n\n4. **Conclusion**:\n   - Option B does not provide direct support for the argument about the effectiveness of exercise in quitting smoking. It only provides information about the sample size, which is not relevant to the core argument.\nThe correct option is:"]
